WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.840
What if I told you the world’s “first
computer programmer” was born over two hundred

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:05.840
years ago?

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.740
And, what if that programmer was a she.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:12.750
Lady Ada Lovelace was her name -- and her
genius was nearly lost to history.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:18.160
Daughter to the famous Romantic poet Lord
Byron, Ada was almost relegated as a footnote

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:19.359
in her father’s biography.

00:00:19.359 --> 00:00:24.320
In the nineteenth century, at the height of
the industrial revolution, she was pushed

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.820
into the male dominion of mathematics and
science by her zealot mother.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:33.290
Mentored by the “father of the computer,”
she emerged as a woman far ahead of her time

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:36.950
in her ability to see what could be.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:41.580
Not your typical aristocratic lady, part of
Ada’s story holds a shadowy secret.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:45.530
And, on her deathbed, her last wish speaks
volumes.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:55.270
Today on Biographics we explore the “Enchantress
of Number,” Ada Lovelace.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:01:06.830
Early Life

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:12.620
Ada Lovelace was born Augusta Ada Byron in
London on December 10, 1815 to the philandering

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:17.610
Romantic poet Lord Byron and strictly religious
Annabella Milbanke.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:21.080
Ada was Lord Byron’s only legitimate child.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.640
Lord and Lady Byron were members of the high
British society.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.740
Beyond social status, they shared virtually
nothing in common.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:34.740
Annabella was analytical and conformist while
Lord Byron cared little for numbers and logic.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:41.240
True, he was a celebrated and adored poet,
yet his behavior raised more than a few eyebrows.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:46.380
He was known to drink from a human skull,
own a pet bear, and engage in numerous love

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.990
affairs with both men and women.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:52.330
One notorious scandal involved an affair with
his own half-sister.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:59.090
A scorned lover once said of Lord Byron, he
was “mad, bad, and dangerous to know.”

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:04.000
Not surprisingly, Annabella and Lord Byron’s
marriage did not last beyond a year.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.889
In a contemptuous split when Ada was only
five weeks old, Lord Byron left the home.

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:12.670
He wrote of this parting, “Is thy face like
thy mother's my fair child!

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:13.670
ADA!

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:16.110
sole daughter of my house and heart?”

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:20.450
Deeply bitter and resentful, Lady Annabella
covered his portrait with a large curtain

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:23.010
and forbade Ada from ever looking at it.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:27.980
Four months after Lord Byron left his infant
daughter he left England for good, never to

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:28.980
return.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.799
When Ada was eight years old, he died of an
illness in Greece while fighting in the war

00:02:31.799 --> 00:02:32.799
of independence.

00:02:32.799 --> 00:02:34.440
He was 36 years old.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:39.440
Although Ada never met her father, she remained
fascinated with him and his poetry over the

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:40.939
course of her life.

00:02:40.939 --> 00:02:46.459
Annabella was determined to steer her daughter
away from developing her father’s "volatile

00:02:46.459 --> 00:02:48.569
poetic insanity" as she called it.

00:02:48.569 --> 00:02:52.359
She truly believed Lord Byron was mentally
ill.

00:02:52.359 --> 00:02:58.769
Almost to the brink of fanaticism, Annabella
saw to it that Ada received an education rigorous

00:02:58.769 --> 00:03:00.609
in mathematics and science.

00:03:00.609 --> 00:03:05.549
This was a rather unusual course of study
for girls in British Victorian society even

00:03:05.549 --> 00:03:08.739
if Annabella herself was gifted in math.

00:03:08.739 --> 00:03:12.809
Byron referred to her as the “Princess of
Parallelograms.”

00:03:12.809 --> 00:03:16.909
Popular opinion of the time favored one of
Ada’s later tutors and brilliant mathematician,

00:03:16.909 --> 00:03:21.650
Augustus De Morgan, who believed women were
incapable of excelling in the discipline.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:26.580
On the one hand, De Morgan acknowledged Ada’s
abilities yet in a letter to her mother he

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:31.090
wrote, “The very great tension of mind which
they require is beyond the strength of a woman's

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:33.739
physical power of application.”

00:03:33.739 --> 00:03:38.129
Basically meaning, women were incapable because
they were not as strong as men.

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:40.449
Ada would prove that theory wrong.

00:03:40.449 --> 00:03:45.260
As a young girl and into her teen years Ada
was mostly isolated and did not spend a great

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:46.939
deal of time with her mother.

00:03:46.939 --> 00:03:51.849
Still, Annabella was committed to her daughter’s
education and well-being.

00:03:51.849 --> 00:03:57.900
She employed some of the greatest intellectual
minds to tutor Ada including William King,

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:03.359
a physician, William Frend, a social reformer
and Mary Somerville, a Scottish astronomer,

00:04:03.359 --> 00:04:08.230
mathematician, and the first woman to be admitted
into the Royal Astronomical Society.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.459
Ada and Somerville became close friends.

00:04:10.459 --> 00:04:15.709
In addition to algebra and geometry, Ada was
taught lessons in history, literature, languages,

00:04:15.709 --> 00:04:18.850
geography, music, sewing and shorthand.

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:24.409
These were all common subject areas but Ada’s
mother planned something else that was far

00:04:24.409 --> 00:04:25.409
more bizarre.

00:04:25.409 --> 00:04:29.690
In an attempt to protect her daughter from
developing her father’s impulses, Ada had

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:32.570
to lie still for long periods of time.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:35.240
This was a lesson in self-control.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:38.300
Ada was a bright student and showed promise
for math and science.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:42.850
At the age of 12 after a year-long tour of
Europe, she became obsessed with birds and

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:43.850
flight.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:49.340
She researched what she referred to as ““flyology,”
and imaginatively and methodically conceptualized

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:51.940
a flying machine that could flap its wings.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:56.180
She wrote to her mother, “I have got a scheme,
to make a thing in the form of a horse with

00:04:56.180 --> 00:05:01.650
a steam engine in the inside so contrived
as to move an immense pair of wings, fixed

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:06.129
on the outside of the horse, in such a manner
as to carry it up into the air while a person

00:05:06.129 --> 00:05:07.570
sits on its back.”

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:11.930
For her design, she considered the type of
material -- paper, silk, or feathers; navigational

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:15.220
equipment -- a compass; and steam -- the source
of power.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:20.240
In between throwing herself into intellectual
pursuits, Ada battled sickness.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:23.840
She frequently complained of headaches that
obscured her vision.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:28.169
In 1829, she contracted measles, a common
childhood affliction in the 1800s.

00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:33.210
The illness resulted in continuous bed rest
for a year and she was disabled temporarily.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:38.629
In 1831 at the age of 16, she was finally
able to walk again with the aid of crutches.

00:05:38.629 --> 00:05:43.069
By the age of 17 Ada was feeling better and
in keeping with the customs of her social

00:05:43.069 --> 00:05:48.610
class, she was presented at Court and became
became a popular belle of the season -- dancing

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:56.680
and charming the attendees with her “brilliant
mind.”

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:02.440
Spark of Genius

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:06.509
The industrial revolution was in full force
by the time Ada was a teenager.

00:06:06.509 --> 00:06:10.590
It was a glorious time in history for the
advancement of technology, and the perfect

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:13.639
time for the inquisitive Ada to be coming
of age.

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:18.660
Make no mistake, she had the makings of a
phenom (inherit abilities combined with rigorous

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:19.689
education).

00:06:19.689 --> 00:06:24.800
She also had access to intellects, inventors,
and influential people in power all because

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:26.699
of her position in society.

00:06:26.699 --> 00:06:31.690
All of this was the perfect storm and meeting
the famed inventor Charles Babbage was Ada’s

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:32.690
lightning bolt.

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:38.069
In 1833, the seventeen year old Ada was among
a select group to attend a coveted party hosted

00:06:38.069 --> 00:06:41.800
by Babbage, know by many today as the “father
of the computer.”

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:45.820
A guest wrote at the time, “One of the three
qualifications for those who sought to be

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:48.860
invited were intellect, beauty, or rank.”

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:51.160
Ada fit right in.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:56.199
At the soiree, Babbage unveiled a small part
of his latest machine, a massive mechanical

00:06:56.199 --> 00:06:59.400
calculator known as the Difference Engine.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:05.090
The design for Babbage’s engine was revolutionary
and fully constructed, would perform the work

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:07.419
of an army of men crunching numbers.

00:07:07.419 --> 00:07:12.050
The machine would be powered by steam and
would methodically perform complex calculations

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:17.659
using only addition, by breaking it into smaller
and smaller pieces -- known as the method

00:07:17.659 --> 00:07:18.659
of finite differences.

00:07:18.659 --> 00:07:21.590
Then, it would print out the values into a
table.

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:25.700
Babbage’s machine had enormous potential
in the nineteenth century since tables were

00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:30.150
used in many areas including navigation, astronomy
and engineering.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:33.610
With this powerful calculator, human error
would be erased.

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:37.020
The engine was accurate within thirty one
decimal places.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:40.699
To many guests that night Babbage’s invention
was a hunk of metal and little more than a

00:07:40.699 --> 00:07:42.430
party favor.

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:43.620
Not to Ada.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:47.599
De Morgan’s wife was there, and later wrote
of the night, “When most of the guests looked

00:07:47.599 --> 00:07:52.550
on with the expression that savages show on
seeing a looking glass, Miss Byron, young

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:57.259
as she was, understood its working and saw
the great beauty of the invention.”

00:07:57.259 --> 00:08:01.250
Babbage must have been impressed with Ada’s
intellect and enthusiasm for the machine.

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:07.610
From that night on, he remained her mentor
and lifelong friend.

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:13.379
Analytical Engine

00:08:13.379 --> 00:08:19.439
Babbage’s Difference Engine would not come
to be after a disagreement with his engineer

00:08:19.439 --> 00:08:20.720
Joseph Clement.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:24.909
In those days, by law, the engineer owned
the drawings and after their falling out,

00:08:24.909 --> 00:08:26.479
he could not get them back.

00:08:26.479 --> 00:08:29.979
Consequently, the British government also
withdrew their funding for the project.

00:08:29.979 --> 00:08:34.730
This forced Babbage back to his original concept
and he came up with an even better idea in

00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:39.650
1834 -- a far more complex machine he called
the Analytical Engine.

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:45.070
The Analytical Engine is considered to be
the world’s first programmable general-purpose

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:46.070
computer.

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:49.630
The basic structure of the engine is essentially
the same as modern-day computers.

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:52.730
Of course, Babbage’s machine would have
been massive in scale.

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:58.570
His entry-level machines would have been 45
feet long by 15 feet high but he talked about

00:08:58.570 --> 00:09:01.460
others, ten times the size.

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:05.089
Nothing like it had ever been conceived or
attempted to be built.

00:09:05.089 --> 00:09:08.410
In fact, in his lifetime, only a small trial
engine was constructed.

00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:13.610
The Analytical Engine consisted of four components:
the mill, which calculated units; the store,

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:18.490
where the data was held for processing; and
the reader and printer, the input and output

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:19.490
devices.

00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:23.640
It was truly groundbreaking and would become
Ada’s legacy.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:28.200
Since Ada and Babbage’s first meeting, she
became his protege; he was the teacher and

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:29.560
she was the pupil.

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:33.030
After Babbage showed her the plans for the
Analytical Engine, she eagerly went on a tour

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:37.210
of cotton mills in the north of England to
see the most technologically advanced machine

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:39.670
of the day -- the Jacquard loom.

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:45.030
The loom automated weaving of patterned silk
and was controlled by a series of punch cards.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:47.280
It was fascinating and also controversial.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:52.890
A cotton mill with a Jacquard loom didn’t
need skilled workers to weave intricate patterns.

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:57.530
While groups such as the Luddites were protesting
against these machines on account of replacing

00:09:57.530 --> 00:10:01.530
workers’ jobs, Ada was thinking something
else entirely.

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:06.170
She was deeply interested in the genius behind
the punch cards and wanted to know how men

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:09.910
were translating the complicated patterns
into something simple the loom could understand.

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:14.350
She saw the similarities to her beloved machine
and later wrote, “The Analytical Engine

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:19.180
weaves algebraic patterns just as the Jacquard
loom weaves flowers and leaves.”

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:24.410
For the next ten years, in between getting
married and having three children, Ada focused

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:29.240
all her energy into learning everything she
could about the Analytical Engine.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:33.750
She wrote, “I think I am more determined
than ever in my future plans, and I have quite

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:37.340
made up my mind that nothing must be suffered
to interfere with them.

00:10:37.340 --> 00:10:41.430
I intend to make such arrangements in town
as will secure me a couple of hours daily

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:44.470
(with very few exceptions) for my studies.”

00:10:44.470 --> 00:10:48.280
After attending one of Babbage’s rare lectures,
a military engineer and future Italian prime

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:53.710
minister Luigi Menabrea wrote an impressive
article, Sketch of the analytical engine invented

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:55.340
by Charles Babbage Esquir.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:59.600
The paper was extremely detailed and mathematical
-- and it was also written in French.

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:03.390
Ada decided to translate the paper and, since
she knew the engine so well she would add

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:04.660
her own thoughts to it.

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:09.220
Many years later Babbage claimed to have told
Ada to translate it and write her own account,

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:11.750
and she responded by saying she hadn’t thought
of it.

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:16.070
Either way, she went about the task “like
a devil possessed,” and when she was finished,

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:19.070
her notes were three times as long as the
original paper.

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:23.860
Ada published her completed article in Scientific
Memoirs, an English scientific journal published

00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:26.440
by Richard Taylor in 1843.

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:31.421
Ada used only her initials “A.A.L.,” for
Augusta Ada Lovelace, in the publication.

00:11:31.421 --> 00:11:34.810
The part of the paper that gives weight to
Ada’s credit as “first computer programmer”

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:40.090
comes in section G. In it, Ada wrote of how
the engine could be programmed with a code

00:11:40.090 --> 00:11:45.520
to calculate Bernoulli numbers -- an algorithm
to be carried out by a machine and thus the

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:47.230
first computer program.

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:51.880
Along with numbers, Ada described how codes
could be created for the device to handle

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:53.330
letters and symbols.

00:11:53.330 --> 00:11:58.100
She also theorized a method for the engine
to repeat a series of instructions, or looping,

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:00.760
a process computer programs use today.

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:05.720
Ada also offered up other concepts in her
paper, such as thoughts on artificial intelligence.

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:10.220
She wrote, “The Analytical Engine has no
pretensions whatever to originate anything.

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:12.610
It can do whatever we know how to order it
to perform.

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:17.740
It can follow analysis; but it has no power
of anticipating any analytical relations or

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:18.740
truths.”

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:23.930
Her notes were significant in that they captured
Ada’s vision for the Analytical Engine.

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:28.600
And this is where she exceeds her mentor in
understanding its full potential.

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:30.180
Babbage’s historian wrote:

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:33.730
“Ada saw something that Babbage in some
sense failed to see.

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:39.070
In Babbage's world his engines were bound
by number...What Lovelace saw...was that number

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:44.670
could represent entities other than quantity….looking
back from the present high ground of modern

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:49.330
computing, if we are looking and sifting history
for that transition, then that transition

00:12:49.330 --> 00:12:53.810
was made explicitly by Ada in that 1843 paper.”

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:57.740
Babbage was impressed with Ada and wrote,
“The more I read your notes, the more surprised

00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:02.990
I am and regret not having earlier explored
so rich a vein of the noblest metal.”

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:07.190
The next obvious step for the Analytical Engine
would have been Babbage and Ada’s crowning

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:09.500
moment, but it would not come to pass.

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:13.870
Babbage was known to be a difficult man and
he argued with politicians over his machines.

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:19.030
The relationship with Parliament was particularly
sour after Babbage failed to deliver his government

00:13:19.030 --> 00:13:20.330
funded, Difference Engine.

00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:24.940
The loss of money in those days was the equivalent
of two Royal Navy warships.

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:29.030
Ada had a plan to promote the engine but it
was a sensitive subject -- one she broached

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:31.970
in a letter dated August 14, 1843.

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:36.080
She asked Babbage if she could take over for
him in promoting the Analytical Engine while

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:38.720
he step aside and focus only on building it.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:43.570
She ended with, “You will wonder over this
last query, but I strongly advise you not

00:13:43.570 --> 00:13:44.670
to reject it.”

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:47.320
Babbage said no, refusing all of her conditions.

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:50.490
He would not give up control and likely saw
her asking, and presuming she could raise

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:54.140
the money, as an audacious move.

00:13:54.140 --> 00:13:59.820
Personal Life

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:07.600
Depending on the lens used to evaluate Ada,
she can be hailed as a genius, a Victorian

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:11.830
high society lady, a mother and wife, a gambler,
adulteress, or drug addict.

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:14.180
In truth, she may have been all of these things.

00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:17.940
She was like most of us -- complex and contradictory.

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:20.940
As one would expect, Ada married and had children.

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:26.200
She wed William King (not William King her
tutor) in 1835 at the age of 19.

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:27.200
He was 30.

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:31.450
King seemed to be a “precise, conscientious
and decent man, if somewhat stiff.”

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:35.380
Three years after they tied the knot, King
became the Earl of Lovelace and Ada took the

00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:37.390
title Countess of Lovelace.

00:14:37.390 --> 00:14:42.480
They had three children together: Byron, born
in May 1836; Anne Isabella, called Annabella,

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:47.160
born in September 1837; and Ralph Gordon,
born in July 1839.

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:52.680
For a time between 1835 and 1839 Ada focused
most of energy on running the large household

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:58.100
though she found time for horse riding, learning
the harp and studying mathematics.

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:02.510
After their third child was born, Ada turned
her attention back to maths and sciences,

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:05.320
and was tutored by Augustus De Morgan.

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:06.320
King was supportive.

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:11.440
Socially, the couple had many prominent friends
and acquaintances including the writer Charles

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:12.440
Dickens.

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:17.100
In addition to headaches and measles suffered
in childhood, Ada had recurrent health problems

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:18.100
as an adult.

00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:22.800
After a bout of cholera in 1837 she had problems
with asthma and her digestive system.

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:26.510
She was prescribed the powerfully addictive
painkiller laudanum, an opiate, to be taken

00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:27.510
with wine.

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:31.980
The drug altered her personality and she reported
hallucinations and mood swings.

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:37.390
In 1841, Ada and Medora Leigh, the daughter
of Lord Byron's half-sister Augusta Leigh,

00:15:37.390 --> 00:15:41.230
were told by Ada's mother that Lord Byron
was also Medora's father.

00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:44.750
In February, Ada wrote to her mother: “I
am not in the least astonished.

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:50.170
In fact, you merely confirm what I have for
years and years felt scarcely a doubt about,

00:15:50.170 --> 00:15:55.510
but should have considered it most improper
in me to hint to you that I in any way suspected.”

00:15:55.510 --> 00:16:00.320
She did not blame the incestuous relationship
on her father, but instead blamed Augusta

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:04.470
Leigh: “I fear she is more inherently wicked
than he ever was.”

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:06.820
In the 1840s Ada flirted with scandals.

00:16:06.820 --> 00:16:10.970
First, from a relaxed relationship with men
who were not her husband, which led to rumours

00:16:10.970 --> 00:16:12.760
she was having affairs.

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:14.920
And then, an old family vice surfaced.

00:16:14.920 --> 00:16:20.030
Ada’s life took a shadowy turn in 1851 when
she began gambling on horses.

00:16:20.030 --> 00:16:25.040
Perhaps she was trying in vain to raise funds
for her beloved Analytical Engine but no one

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:26.350
really knows.

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:31.250
Ada did not act alone, she was part of a gambling
ring and in the spring season, her bets went

00:16:31.250 --> 00:16:32.250
horribly wrong.

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:36.640
It has been said, Ada may have devised a mathematical
scheme to predict the winning horses.

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:38.740
If so, it didn’t work out well for her.

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:43.540
In a failed bet at the Epsom Derby, Ada ended
up owing the equivalent of roughly half a

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:44.540
million pounds.

00:16:44.540 --> 00:16:47.980
To get out of her debt, she pawned some of
the family jewels.

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:51.230
In 1852, Ada became gravely ill and took to
her bed.

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:58.290
She died painfully and slowly of uterine cancer
on November 27, 1852 at the age of 36 -- the

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:00.130
exact age her father had been.

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:03.680
A few months before she died, she made an
unknown confession to her husband that made

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:04.790
him leave her bedside.

00:17:04.790 --> 00:17:08.000
Her final wish was an act of defiance against
her mother.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:12.530
Ada wanted to be buried next to the man who
loomed large in her life but she never knew...her

00:17:12.530 --> 00:17:13.530
father.

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:17.500
Ada’s body was taken miles away from her
home to the Byron family vault inside the

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:24.740
Church of St. Mary Magdalene in the small
English town of Hucknall.

00:17:24.740 --> 00:17:29.360
Ada’s Legacy

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:36.410
“That brain of mine is something more than
merely mortal, as time will show.”

00:17:36.410 --> 00:17:41.890
Ada’s contributions were buried for 100
years...discovered by a scientist, Alan Turing,

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:44.340
in the 1940s during the Second World War.

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:48.560
Turing was something of a kindred spirit who
who was interested in the same things she

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:51.630
was -- machines that could act upon instruction.

00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:55.890
His work ultimately led the effort to build
a machine with the code name, The Bombe that

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:59.390
deciphered encrypted messages sent by Hitler’s
armed forces.

00:17:59.390 --> 00:18:03.600
Turing’s work had begun before reading Ada’s
notes on the Analytical Engine yet he was

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:05.180
greatly influenced by them.

00:18:05.180 --> 00:18:09.530
She, along with Babbage, essentially paved
the way for Turing who is considered today

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:13.770
as the father of theoretical computer science
and artificial intelligence.

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:17.080
Consequently, Ada’s notes surfaced for the
world in B.V.

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:21.440
Bowden’s book “Faster Than Thought: A
Symposium on Digital Computing Machines”

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:22.440
in 1953.

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:28.210
Then, during the 1970s, the U.S. Department
of Defense developed a high-order computer

00:18:28.210 --> 00:18:33.490
programming language to supersede the hundreds
of different ones then in use by the military.

00:18:33.490 --> 00:18:35.500
It was named “Ada.”

00:18:35.500 --> 00:18:39.610
Ada is still used around the world today in
the operation of real-time systems in the

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:44.500
aviation, health care, transportation, financial,
infrastructure and space industries.

00:18:44.500 --> 00:18:48.990
In the present day, an international celebration
takes place on the second Tuesday of October

00:18:48.990 --> 00:18:51.340
-- Ada Lovelace Day.

00:18:51.340 --> 00:18:56.160
Founded in 2009 by Suw Charman-Anderson, it’s
aim is to recognize the achievements of women

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:59.160
in science, technology, engineering and maths
(STEM).

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:03.620
It also has a goal to increase the profile
of women in STEM and, in doing so, create

00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:07.400
new role models who will encourage more girls
into STEM careers.

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:11.230
Ada is everywhere in our modern digital world
-- immortalized on the internet forever in

00:19:11.230 --> 00:19:16.810
news articles, blog posts, memes, quotable
quotes...there’s even some controversy of

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:20.300
whether or not she deserves the claim “first
computer programmer.”

00:19:20.300 --> 00:19:25.170
But, there is no denying her vision far surpassed
any of her contemporaries, including Babbage.

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:29.930
In all his 11 volumes of published writings
he never wrote of the aspirations for computing

00:19:29.930 --> 00:19:30.930
like Ada had.

00:19:30.930 --> 00:19:35.420
“Those who have learned to walk on the threshold
of the unknown worlds, by means of what are

00:19:35.420 --> 00:19:41.670
commonly termed par excellence the exact sciences,
may then, with the fair white wings of imagination,

00:19:41.670 --> 00:20:27.049
hope to soar further into the unexplored amidst
which we live.”

